// %% [markdown]
// # Versioned weather: an Icechunk repo, four refs, one type system
//
// [Icechunk](https://icechunk.io) is a transactional storage engine over
// Zarr: a repo of **immutable snapshots** with git-like branches and tags,
// and exactly one mutable file (the ref table). This notebook reads one —
// `examples/data/station_temps.icechunk`, a small station-temperature grid
// written by `examples/tools/make_station_icechunk.fsx` — through Blade's
// icechunk provider.
//
// What Blade does differently with a versioned store:
//
// - **Everything resolves at compile time.** A checkout pins ref → snapshot
//   → manifests → per-chunk byte ranges while the program compiles; the
//   generated executable does raw offset reads with no Icechunk logic in it
//   at all. Immutability is what makes that sound.
// - **Provenance is exact.** Compile-time folds print the snapshot id their
//   bytes came from — not a file hash, the actual commit identity.
// - **Refusals are features.** Two checkouts may only mix where their axes
//   are provably the same axis; anything else refuses at compile time.
//
// The repo's history is authored for the story: tag `v1.0` is the raw
// ingest, with a +1.5 K warm bias in the eastern third of the domain;
// branch `main` is the bias-correction commit — a *data-only* change whose
// coordinate arrays are byte-identical to v1.0's; branch `regrid` rewrites
// the latitude axis at twice the resolution; and `release` exists as both
// a branch *and* a tag, deliberately.
//
// %%
import icechunk as ic

// The path is relative to THIS FILE: a notebook cell evaluates with the
// notebook's own directory as its working directory.
// Running the same code as a plain .blade from the repo root instead wants
// `examples/data/station_temps.icechunk`.
let repo = ic.load("data/station_temps.icechunk")

// %% [markdown]
// `repo` is a **repo handle** — it has no variables, because "the data" is
// not a meaningful notion until a ref says *which* data. `checkout` is the
// factory: give it a branch, tag, or snapshot id, and the result is a typed
// store whose dims and vars come from that snapshot's metadata.
//
// %%
let now = repo.checkout("main")
let temp_now = now.vars.temp |> ic.read
let mean_now = reduce(temp_now, (+), axes = 3) / 2880.0
mean_now

// %% [markdown]
// A compile-time fold announces exactly which commit fed it: watch the
// `[provenance] folded ... snapshot ...` line — that is the snapshot id,
// straight from the ref resolution, not a checksum.
//
// Note what a folded array *is*. `let static` asks for a value the compiler
// can hold, and the compile-time value language has no array carrier, so the
// cells arrive as a structural **tuple** -- the staging model, not a quirk of
// this provider. The array path is the plain `|> ic.read` above. So fold small
// things: this two-cell latitude-bounds pair reads nicely, where folding the
// field itself would hand back a 2880-element tuple.
//
// %%
let static lat_span = ic.read(now.vars.lat_bounds)
lat_span

// %% [markdown]
// Branches and tags are **separate namespaces** in Icechunk, so a bare name
// resolves only while it is unique. The provider module ships three
// unit-typed markers — `ic.branch`, `ic.tag`, `ic.snapshot` — and
// `checkout` dispatches on the marker's *unit*: same method, three
// namespaces, disambiguated by type rather than by string prefixes.
//
// %%
let v1 = repo.checkout("v1.0", ic.tag)
let temp_v1 = v1.vars.temp |> ic.read

// %% [markdown]
// ## The headline: arithmetic across checkouts
//
// `temp_now` and `temp_v1` come from **different commits** — yet subtracting
// them typechecks. That is not a loophole; it is a proof obligation the
// compiler discharged from metadata alone: between the two snapshots, the
// `time`/`lat`/`lon` coordinate arrays have the same stable node ids, the
// same metadata bytes, and the same chunk tables — no diff — so each axis
// is *the same axis*, and the two arrays share its index type. Only the
// data changed, which is exactly what the subtraction is for.
//
// The bias band is 4 of 12 longitudes at +1.5 K, so the mean drift is
// −0.5 K exactly.
//
// %%
let drift = temp_now - temp_v1
let mean_drift = reduce(drift, (+), axes = 3) / 2880.0
mean_drift
// EXPECT: mean_drift = -0.5

// %% [markdown]
// A tag can move only by deliberate re-tagging; a snapshot id can never
// move — the id *is* a truncated hash of the snapshot's own contents, so
// the same twenty characters can never be handed to a different dataset.
// Pinning one makes a program bit-exactly reproducible — the compiled
// binary reads those chunks or fails loudly, forever.
//
// %%
let pinned = repo.checkout("9ZDMT4JXK0A92JRXYXRG", ic.snapshot)
let temp_pin = pinned.vars.temp |> ic.read
reduce(temp_pin - temp_v1, (+), axes = 3)
// EXPECT: 0 exactly -- the tag v1.0 points at this very snapshot

// %% [markdown]
// ## Refusal one: the regridded branch
//
// `regrid` rewrote the latitude axis at twice the resolution. Its `temp` is
// a perfectly good array — but its lat axis is *not the same axis* as
// `main`'s, so mixing them refuses at compile time. **This cell fails on
// purpose**: the point is the error, an index-type mismatch naming the two
// incompatible axes, instead of a silently wrong broadcast.
//
// %%
let rg = repo.checkout("regrid")
let bad = (rg.vars.temp |> ic.read) - temp_now

// %% [markdown]
// ## Refusal two: the ambiguous name
//
// `release` names a branch *and* a tag — legal in Icechunk, since the two
// are separate namespaces. A bare checkout refuses rather than guess.
// **This cell fails on purpose**; the message names both candidates and the
// marker spellings that resolve them.
//
// %%
let rel = repo.checkout("release")

// %% [markdown]
// The remedy is the marker — and the tag `release` turns out to be the raw
// snapshot again:
//
// %%
let rel_tag = repo.checkout("release", ic.tag)
let rel_temp = rel_tag.vars.temp |> ic.read
reduce(rel_temp - temp_v1, (+), axes = 3)
// EXPECT: 0 exactly -- tag release = v1.0's snapshot

// %% [markdown]
// ## What just happened
//
// Four refs, one type system: `main` and `v1.0` interoperate because their
// axes carry no diff; `regrid` refuses because its axis genuinely diverged;
// the ambiguous `release` refuses until a unit marker names its namespace;
// and the pinned snapshot will read identically for as long as the commit
// exists. Every one of those decisions was made at compile time, from
// metadata alone — the running program only ever executed straight-line
// chunk reads.
//
// To poke at the store itself, regenerate it with
// `dotnet fsi examples/tools/make_station_icechunk.fsx` and try rewriting
// a coordinate array in one snapshot: the headline cell above will refuse,
// and it will tell you which axis diverged.
